-- title:
--   Pinsketch decoder I: the word-RAM machine model and its state predicates
-- statement:
--   This bundle fixes the deterministic word-RAM model in which the Pinsketch decoder is written, and the vocabulary used to state its contracts.
--
--   A program is a finite sequence of statements over a small instruction set: immediate loads, register moves and additions, indexed loads and stores, comparisons and conditional jumps. Also defined are:
--   1. the statement and condition syntax, together with a compiler that turns structured statements into the flat instruction list executed by `Sem`;
--   2. the predicate `At P pc c`, asserting that the code stored at program address `pc` is the compiled form of `c`;
--   3. the big-step relation `Sem P E pc k s k' s'`, asserting that program `P`, started at address `pc` in RAM state `s` with field-data environment `E`, halts after at most `k` steps in state `s'` with program counter at most `k'` (the `boundaryState` normal form);
--   4. the memory predicates `MemoryFrame`, `FieldArrayStored` and `PolynomialStored`, which describe which addresses a stage may modify and how a field array or a polynomial is laid out in memory.
--
--   This is the interface layer: it contains no algorithmic content, but every decoder specification and every composition of the four decoder stages is expressed in this vocabulary.
-- source:
--   arXiv:2611.11409, Section 2.2 (Pinsketch) and Appendix C (decoder implementation); Dodis, Ostrovsky, Reyzin, Smith, Fuzzy Extractors: How to Generate Strong Keys from Biometrics and Other Noisy Data, SIAM J. Comput. 38(1), 2008, Section 6.3

